      "title": "Shift the record; the environment stays",
      "html": "<figure class=\"volume6\" data-v6=\"V41\" id=\"visual-V41\" lang=\"en\"><header><span>V41</span><h3>Shift the record; the environment stays</h3></header><p class=\"v6-intro\">After shifting the record, what random information can still remain?</p><div class=\"v5-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-env=\"25\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Θ=1/4</button><button type=\"button\" data-env=\"75\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Θ=3/4</button></div><div class=\"v5-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-offset=\"0\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Start at 0</button><button type=\"button\" data-offset=\"3\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Start at 3</button><button type=\"button\" data-offset=\"6\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Start at 6</button></div><p class=\"v6-compact-result\">Mixture mean 1/2 is not every path’s limit</p><div class=\"v6-pair\"><section><h4>Move a finite window</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume6-v1/V41-en-25-0-0.svg\" alt=\"Move a finite window\" data-pane=\"0\"></section><section><h4>What the long run retains</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume6-v1/V41-en-25-0-1.svg\" alt=\"What the long run retains\" data-pane=\"1\"></section></div><output data-result aria-live=\"polite\">Mixture mean 1/2; the long-run mean in this environment is 1/4。</output><p class=\"v6-limit\">The finite window is illustrative. Θ is the conditional parameter, not the frequency of these six entries.</p><details><summary>Model assumptions and mathematical limits</summary><p>Θ is 1/4 or 3/4 with equal probabilities; given Θ, the Xk are iid Bernoulli(Θ). Displayed windows are deterministic examples, not simulations or a proof of the strong law; finite frequencies are not limits. The conditional strong law gives limit Θ, while the mixture mean is 1/2. Here the invariant sigma-field is sigma(Θ), modulo null sets. Controls move a finite window or compare conditional environments; they do not resample Θ along one path. General Birkhoff requires measure preservation and integrability; ergodicity makes the conditional mean constant but does not imply mixing.</p></details><figcaption id=\"visual-V41-end\">The diagram explains a specific model in the text; full theorems and proofs remain in the chapter and its foundations.</figcaption><nav><a href=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume6-v1/V41.en.svg\">SVG</a><a href=\"/print/V41.en.html\">Static print</a></nav></figure>",
      "condition": "Θ is 1/4 or 3/4 with equal probabilities; given Θ, the Xk are iid Bernoulli(Θ). Displayed windows are deterministic examples, not simulations or a proof of the strong law; finite frequencies are not limits. The conditional strong law gives limit Θ, while the mixture mean is 1/2. Here the invariant sigma-field is sigma(Θ), modulo null sets. Controls move a finite window or compare conditional environments; they do not resample Θ along one path. General Birkhoff requires measure preservation and integrability; ergodicity makes the conditional mean constant but does not imply mixing.",
