      "title": "A new table need not mean a new value",
      "html": "<figure class=\"volume6\" data-v6=\"V44\" id=\"visual-V44\" lang=\"en\"><header><span>V44</span><h3>A new table need not mean a new value</h3></header><p class=\"v6-intro\">Seat first, then label each table. Do these operations define the same partition?</p><div class=\"v5-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-label=\"atomic\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Atomic H: labels may tie</button><button type=\"button\" data-label=\"diffuse\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Nonatomic H</button></div><div class=\"v5-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-seat=\"A\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Join A: 1/2</button><button type=\"button\" data-seat=\"B\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Join B: 1/4</button><button type=\"button\" data-seat=\"new\" aria-pressed=\"true\">New table: 1/4</button></div><p class=\"v6-compact-result\">New table ≠ new value: tied labels merge tables</p><div class=\"v6-pair\"><section><h4>Tables: copy ancestry</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume6-v1/V44-en-atomic-new-0.svg\" alt=\"Tables: copy ancestry\" data-pane=\"0\"></section><section><h4>Observations: equal labels</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume6-v1/V44-en-atomic-new-1.svg\" alt=\"Observations: equal labels\" data-pane=\"1\"></section></div><output data-result aria-live=\"polite\">New-table chance is 1/4; a new value also depends on H. The tied labels shown are one positive-probability configuration.</output><details><summary>Model assumptions and mathematical limits</summary><p>Standard one-parameter CRP with alpha=1. Given A={1,2}, B={3}, customer 4 joins A, B or a new table with probabilities 1/2, 1/4, 1/4. Table labels are iid H and independent of seating. For H=(delta_z+delta_w)/2, the displayed all-z labels form a positive-probability configuration, not an almost-sure outcome. Nonatomic H gives distinct labels almost surely. Buttons inspect conditional branches, not equiprobable alternatives. Equality of observed values may coarsen the table partition. P(1~Pi2)=1/2, whereas this atomic H gives P(X1=X2)=3/4.</p></details><figcaption id=\"visual-V44-end\">The diagram explains a specific model in the text; full theorems and proofs remain in the chapter and its foundations.</figcaption><nav><a href=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume6-v1/V44.en.svg\">SVG</a><a href=\"/print/V44.en.html\">Static print</a></nav></figure>",
      "condition": "Standard one-parameter CRP with alpha=1. Given A={1,2}, B={3}, customer 4 joins A, B or a new table with probabilities 1/2, 1/4, 1/4. Table labels are iid H and independent of seating. For H=(delta_z+delta_w)/2, the displayed all-z labels form a positive-probability configuration, not an almost-sure outcome. Nonatomic H gives distinct labels almost surely. Buttons inspect conditional branches, not equiprobable alternatives. Equality of observed values may coarsen the table partition. P(1~Pi2)=1/2, whereas this atomic H gives P(X1=X2)=3/4.",
      "asset_base": "/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume6-v1/",
      "status": "author visual review pending",
      "kind": "new explanatory candidate"
