      "title": "Each rotation preserves law; not every selection does",
      "html": "<figure class=\"volume6\" data-v6=\"V45\" id=\"visual-V45\" lang=\"en\"><header><span>V45</span><h3>Each rotation preserves law; not every selection does</h3></header><p class=\"v6-intro\">Every fixed rotation moves uniform mass uniformly. What if the angle is chosen after seeing the point?</p><div class=\"v5-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-rotation=\"0\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Fixed 0°</button><button type=\"button\" data-rotation=\"90\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Fixed 90°</button><button type=\"button\" data-rotation=\"180\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Fixed 180°</button><button type=\"button\" data-rotation=\"adaptive\" aria-pressed=\"false\">See θ, then choose −θ</button></div><div class=\"v6-pair\"><section><h4>Before the map</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume6-v1/V45-en-90-0.svg\" alt=\"Before the map\" data-pane=\"0\"></section><section><h4>After the map</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume6-v1/V45-en-90-1.svg\" alt=\"After the map\" data-pane=\"1\"></section></div><output data-result aria-live=\"polite\">Fixed rotations permute eight masses; current-angle recentering combines all eight into one.</output><details><summary>Model assumptions and mathematical limits</summary><p>Eight equiprobable circle angles with multiples of 90 degrees give an exact finite-group example, not quadrature for continuous Haar measure. Each fixed rotation permutes the eight masses; a(theta)=-theta sends them all to 0. Circle area denotes probability; the concentrated disk has eight times one input mass. The continuous analogue follows from arc-length invariance. Not every data-dependent rotation fails: a radius-based orbit choice may preserve law. Predictable choices for fresh noise require the independence assumptions of section 45.16.</p></details><figcaption id=\"visual-V45-end\">The diagram explains a specific model in the text; full theorems and proofs remain in the chapter and its foundations.</figcaption><nav><a href=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume6-v1/V45.en.svg\">SVG</a><a href=\"/print/V45.en.html\">Static print</a></nav></figure>",
      "condition": "Eight equiprobable circle angles with multiples of 90 degrees give an exact finite-group example, not quadrature for continuous Haar measure. Each fixed rotation permutes the eight masses; a(theta)=-theta sends them all to 0. Circle area denotes probability; the concentrated disk has eight times one input mass. The continuous analogue follows from arc-length invariance. Not every data-dependent rotation fails: a radius-based orbit choice may preserve law. Predictable choices for fresh noise require the independence assumptions of section 45.16.",
      "asset_base": "/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume6-v1/",
      "status": "author visual review pending",
