      "title": "Same row laws, different triangles",
      "html": "<figure class=\"volume6\" data-v6=\"V46\" id=\"visual-V46\" lang=\"en\"><header><span>V46</span><h3>Same row laws, different triangles</h3></header><p class=\"v6-intro\">One row has exactly the same law in two infinite random graphs. Three vertices reveal the difference.</p><div class=\"v5-controls\" role=\"group\"><button type=\"button\" data-array-model=\"pairs\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Independent pair inputs</button><button type=\"button\" data-array-model=\"colors\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Shared vertex colors</button></div><div class=\"v6-controls\"><button data-bit=\"0\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Toggle input 1</button><button data-bit=\"1\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Toggle input 2</button><button data-bit=\"2\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Toggle input 3</button></div><p class=\"v6-compact-result\">All-edge triangle: independent pairs 1/8, vertex colors 0</p><div class=\"v6-pair\"><section><h4>Shared inputs determine edges</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume6-v1/V46-en-colors-011-0.svg\" alt=\"Shared inputs determine edges\" data-pane=\"0\"></section><section><h4>All eight inputs; test a triangle</h4><img src=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume6-v1/V46-en-colors-011-1.svg\" alt=\"All eight inputs; test a triangle\" data-pane=\"1\"></section></div><output data-result aria-live=\"polite\">Each row is iid fair bits; the all-edge triangle probabilities are 1/8 and 0.</output><details><summary>Model assumptions and mathematical limits</summary><p>Infinite symmetric undirected arrays with zero diagonal. A assigns independent fair bits to unordered pairs. B assigns iid fair vertex colors and puts an edge between unlike colors. Each row off the diagonal is iid Bernoulli(1/2) in both models, but the all-edge triangle probabilities are 1/8 and 0. Buttons enumerate three independent input bits: edges in A, vertex colors in B. The lower panel lists all eight equiprobable inputs. Shared vertices organize higher-order constraints; here individual edge pairs are independent. This counterexample does not prove the general Aldous–Hoover theorem; global inputs, separate exchangeability and diagonal mechanisms remain in the text.</p></details><figcaption id=\"visual-V46-end\">The diagram explains a specific model in the text; full theorems and proofs remain in the chapter and its foundations.</figcaption><nav><a href=\"/assets/visuals/explanatory-volume6-v1/V46.en.svg\">SVG</a><a href=\"/print/V46.en.html\">Static print</a></nav></figure>",
      "condition": "Infinite symmetric undirected arrays with zero diagonal. A assigns independent fair bits to unordered pairs. B assigns iid fair vertex colors and puts an edge between unlike colors. Each row off the diagonal is iid Bernoulli(1/2) in both models, but the all-edge triangle probabilities are 1/8 and 0. Buttons enumerate three independent input bits: edges in A, vertex colors in B. The lower panel lists all eight equiprobable inputs. Shared vertices organize higher-order constraints; here individual edge pairs are independent. This counterexample does not prove the general Aldous–Hoover theorem; global inputs, separate exchangeability and diagonal mechanisms remain in the text.",
